From c1ffc97a4b4d92e3e6d3f95c9cad3c32e3408017 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:07:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] AP: End the old association when allowing one after SA Query
 timeout

A STA associated with PMF that loses its association without telling
the AP (it roamed away with FT, went out of range, or dropped its keys)
stays associated and authorized in hostapd until the inactivity timer
removes it. When it returns with Open System or SAE authentication,
hostapd keeps the old entry so that an unprotected frame cannot tear
down a live association, starts the SA Query procedure, and once that
times out allows the new (re)association. The new association was then
processed as a reassociation within the old one:

- add_associated_sta() kept the authorized driver entry and tried to
  update it in place when the old association had been FT, since
  wpa_auth_sta_ft_tk_already_set() still reported the old TK. cfg80211
  accepts supported rates and capabilities only for a station that is
  not yet associated, so NL80211_CMD_SET_STATION failed ("Could not set
  STA to kernel driver") and every attempt was rejected with status 17
  until the inactivity timer expired.
- hostapd_new_assoc_sta() saw a reassociation and sent WPA_REAUTH, which
  with wpa_deny_ptk0_rekey disconnects the STA as a PTK0 rekey.

Once the SA Query has timed out, the STA no longer has the previous
association, so end it there: clear the authorized and associated flags
and stop its accounting session. The association then gets a new driver
STA entry and a new 4-way handshake.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
---
 src/ap/ieee802_11.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/ap/ieee802_11.c b/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
index 219a94b0f..0b840d44c 100644
--- a/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
+++ b/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
@@ -8263,6 +8263,14 @@ static void handle_assoc(struct hostapd_data *hapd,
 		 * unprotected frame would be received by the STA that is now
 		 * trying to associate.
 		 */
+
+		/* The STA no longer has the previous association, so end it
+		 * here: this association then gets a new driver STA entry and
+		 * a new 4-way handshake instead of being processed as a
+		 * reassociation within the old one. */
+		ap_sta_set_authorized(hapd, sta, 0);
+		sta->flags &= ~WLAN_STA_ASSOC;
+		accounting_sta_stop(hapd, sta);
 	}
 
 	/* Make sure that the previously registered inactivity timer will not
-- 
